As Cefiro puts it,

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:53.680
Bay is the new D.W. Griffith
or the anti Griffith,

00:10:53.680 --> 00:10:55.370
as he mixes and matches shots,

00:10:55.370 --> 00:10:58.680
with no concern for
traditional articulations,

00:10:58.680 --> 00:11:00.660
of spatial and temporal continuity.

00:11:00.660 --> 00:11:03.720
Bay's camera never stops
whirling around the carnage,

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:06.740
in ways that flouts standard
filmmaking conventions,

00:11:06.740 --> 00:11:07.980
and the elements within the frame,

00:11:07.980 --> 00:11:09.840
are a flurry of movement too.

00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:14.570
The end result, Bayhem, is
chaotic impressionistic action,

00:11:14.570 --> 00:11:16.190
that defies comprehension.

00:11:16.190 --> 00:11:19.400
In fact, this echoes the main
issue people have with Bay,

00:11:19.400 --> 00:11:22.290
that it's just overwhelming
cinematic noise,

00:11:22.290 --> 00:11:24.930
but opposed to the unconsidered ideology,

00:11:24.930 --> 00:11:25.967
present in Bay's work,

00:11:25.967 --> 00:11:29.860
his action sequences are a
genuine directorial expression,

00:11:29.860 --> 00:11:33.410
used to convey the reflexive
adrenaline clouded state,

00:11:33.410 --> 00:11:36.550
that true danger puts
us in as human beings.

00:11:36.550 --> 00:11:38.640
A couple of these with
the bombastic visuals,

00:11:38.640 --> 00:11:40.560
that comprise Bay's action sequences,

00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:43.530
and you get not only some of
the most striking spectacles,

00:11:43.530 --> 00:11:45.040
in all of blockbuster (indistinct),

00:11:45.040 --> 00:11:46.720
but also something that hits,

00:11:46.720 --> 00:11:48.740
on deeper philosophical questions,

00:11:48.740 --> 00:11:50.910
of the things that captivate us visually.

00:11:50.910 --> 00:11:53.940
We can understand this with
the help of Immanuel Kant,

00:11:53.940 --> 00:11:56.210
who thought there are
two main types of things,

00:11:56.210 --> 00:11:57.960
that give us visual pleasure,

00:11:57.960 --> 00:11:59.670
beauty and the sublime.

00:11:59.670 --> 00:12:00.930
Kant saw beauty as dealing,

00:12:00.930 --> 00:12:03.710
with the contemplation
of order and harmony,

00:12:03.710 --> 00:12:06.020
appreciated for its own sake,

00:12:06.020 --> 00:12:08.450
rather than for its utility or value,

00:12:08.450 --> 00:12:10.950
like a string of symmetrical pearls,

00:12:10.950 --> 00:12:13.480
the gentle swirls of a corkshell,

00:12:13.480 --> 00:12:15.800
or fresh basket of tater tots,

00:12:15.800 --> 00:12:18.250
beautiful things make us feel.

00:12:18.250 --> 00:12:19.790
Take the delicate textures in light,

00:12:19.790 --> 00:12:21.960
in Sofia Coppola in the Virgin Suicides,

00:12:21.960 --> 00:12:24.440
or the set design of Grand Budapest hotel.

00:12:24.440 --> 00:12:27.830
These are beautiful and their
beauty can be comprehended.

00:12:27.830 --> 00:12:29.550
The sublime on the other hand,

00:12:29.550 --> 00:12:32.340
deals with the awe,
inspiring and the dangerous,

00:12:32.340 --> 00:12:33.680
viewed from a place of safety.

00:12:33.680 --> 00:12:36.000
It's the things that we can't comprehend,

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:38.780
because our brains are
just too damn limited,

00:12:38.780 --> 00:12:39.720
or in other words,

00:12:39.720 --> 00:12:42.900
sublimity means the absence
of order or comprehension.

00:12:42.900 --> 00:12:44.340
Sublime fine art,

00:12:44.340 --> 00:12:45.980
a favorite of the romantic era,

00:12:45.980 --> 00:12:48.290
might depict an epic military battle,

00:12:48.290 --> 00:12:51.040
or a ship fighting its
way through the angry sea.

00:12:51.040 --> 00:12:52.630
Through Kant's aesthetic lens,

00:12:52.630 --> 00:12:55.800
Bayhem is a prime example of the supply,

00:12:55.800 --> 00:12:58.950
from giant explosions to
highway spinning car wrecks,

00:12:58.950 --> 00:13:01.550
to New York city being
decimated by meteors,

00:13:01.550 --> 00:13:04.250
the situations that Bay
depict in his action scenes,

00:13:04.250 --> 00:13:06.010
represent levels of destruction,

00:13:06.010 --> 00:13:07.410
that if faced in real life,

00:13:07.410 --> 00:13:09.840
would be so extensive in their impact,

00:13:09.840 --> 00:13:12.010
that we couldn't possibly reckon with it.

00:13:12.010 --> 00:13:15.400
And crucially, the way in which
Bay present these situations

00:13:15.400 --> 00:13:17.530
is geared towards non-comprehension,

00:13:17.530 --> 00:13:19.360
potentially leaving us with a gut feeling,

00:13:19.360 --> 00:13:20.850
of excitement or dread,

00:13:20.850 --> 00:13:24.060
that has no discernible point
of origin within the film.

00:13:24.060 --> 00:13:25.810
It is, as we think the kids would say,

00:13:25.810 --> 00:13:28.870
though we could be
wrong, a whole ass vibe,

00:13:28.870 --> 00:13:30.830
and we aren't alone in thinking so.

00:13:30.830 --> 00:13:32.880
'New York Times' critic A.O. Scott,

00:13:32.880 --> 00:13:36.250
had this to say about Bay's
latest film, Ambulance.

00:13:36.250 --> 00:13:39.260
Bay's virtuosic flouting
of the laws of physics,

00:13:39.260 --> 00:13:41.380
probability and narrative coherence,

00:13:41.380 --> 00:13:44.030
is meant to catapult you
into a zone of sublimity,

00:13:44.030 --> 00:13:47.020
where melodramatic emotion
and adrenalized excitement,

00:13:47.020 --> 00:13:49.970
fuse into a whole new kind of sensation.

00:13:49.970 --> 00:13:53.490
Big, operatic feelings are both
heightened and enlightened,

00:13:53.490 --> 00:13:56.510
by propulsive speed and overscaled action.

00:13:56.510 --> 00:13:58.860
Political theorist William E. Connolly,

00:13:58.860 --> 00:14:00.840
explores the wider overall link,

00:14:00.840 --> 00:14:03.690
between the sublime (indistinct)
style of filmmaking,

00:14:03.690 --> 00:14:06.170
and the ever increasing
speed of capitalism,

00:14:06.170 --> 00:14:08.430
and consumerism in our modern lives.

00:14:08.430 --> 00:14:11.590
Film, to Connolly, is
a useful tool for us,

00:14:11.590 --> 00:14:14.900
to examine human methods of
perception and conception,

00:14:14.900 --> 00:14:18.970
i.e. how we view and think
about stuff that we see.

00:14:18.970 --> 00:14:21.210
In this, the techniques used by directors,

00:14:21.210 --> 00:14:23.550
to evoke emotion, generate engagement,

00:14:23.550 --> 00:14:26.030
and affect the way we
think of the narrative,

00:14:26.030 --> 00:14:28.210
are analogous to the
techniques of systems,

00:14:28.210 --> 00:14:30.110
like capitalism and consumerism.

00:14:30.110 --> 00:14:32.050
Connelly states that increasingly,

00:14:32.050 --> 00:14:34.060
contemporary film is geared more,

00:14:34.060 --> 00:14:36.710
towards what he calls the infraconscious.

00:14:36.710 --> 00:14:39.110
This is the realm of
reflex and impression,

00:14:39.110 --> 00:14:41.400
that Bay's filmmaking does so well,

00:14:41.400 --> 00:14:43.700
and there's nothing inherently sinister,

00:14:43.700 --> 00:14:45.210
about a sublime action film.

00:14:45.210 --> 00:14:47.090
But there is an issue here,

00:14:47.090 --> 00:14:50.940
namely, that Bay's American
excess and his sublime Bayhem,

00:14:50.940 --> 00:14:52.510
aren't mutually exclusive.

00:14:52.510 --> 00:14:55.590
His movies don't stop
evoking the American ideal,

00:14:55.590 --> 00:14:57.940
just because there's a
chaotic action sequence.

00:14:57.940 --> 00:14:59.270
Both are intertwined,

00:14:59.270 --> 00:15:01.750
and Bay seems to sense this intuitively.

00:15:01.750 --> 00:15:04.270
His action scenes often
take little micro breaks,

00:15:04.270 --> 00:15:05.660
to let the audience breathe,

00:15:05.660 --> 00:15:09.100
probably in attempts to alleviate
tension or provide relief.

00:15:09.100 --> 00:15:11.300
Yet, this is where the American excess,

00:15:11.300 --> 00:15:12.700
reliably creeps back in.

00:15:12.700 --> 00:15:15.010
We still get sexy shots of super cars,

00:15:15.010 --> 00:15:18.310
pulled right out of commercials
or weird cringey jokes-

00:15:18.310 --> 00:15:20.871
- If stop covering you it means I'm dead.

00:15:20.871 --> 00:15:21.810
But that ain't gonna happen.

00:15:21.810 --> 00:15:24.720
- [Narrator] Or Macho Man
being almost comically duly,

00:15:24.720 --> 00:15:26.150
often all at once,

00:15:26.150 --> 00:15:29.700
and for Connolly, this connection
might be exactly in tune,

00:15:29.700 --> 00:15:30.840
with what he's diagnosed,

00:15:30.840 --> 00:15:32.870
about the overall direction of filmmaking.

00:15:32.870 --> 00:15:35.680
He argues that the hyper
speed of modern film,

00:15:35.680 --> 00:15:39.130
is a direct reflection
of the ever-growing ways,

00:15:39.130 --> 00:15:41.590
in which capitalism and consumerism,

00:15:41.590 --> 00:15:44.100
seek to overwhelm us as human beings.

00:15:44.100 --> 00:15:46.340
If you've ever been to Time Square,

00:15:46.340 --> 00:15:47.990
you'd probably catch our drift.

00:15:47.990 --> 00:15:50.010
The goal of a flashing neon sign,

00:15:50.010 --> 00:15:52.040
or a billboard the size of a building is,

00:15:52.040 --> 00:15:56.210
like a Michael Bay film to
completely inundate our senses.

00:15:56.210 --> 00:15:57.970
But of course not quite so much,

00:15:57.970 --> 00:15:59.510
that we get too overwhelmed,

00:15:59.510 --> 00:16:01.170
to run into the M and M store,

00:16:01.170 --> 00:16:04.040
and buy a lifetime supply
of blue chocolates.

00:16:04.040 --> 00:16:05.850
Now, we're not saying that there's some,

00:16:05.850 --> 00:16:08.870
conspiracy theory subliminal
messaging going on here,

00:16:08.870 --> 00:16:11.400
or that Michael Bay is a deep state agent,

00:16:11.400 --> 00:16:13.610
brainwashing us into supporting America.

00:16:13.610 --> 00:16:17.260
It's more of an unintended
effect of his visual aesthetic,

00:16:17.260 --> 00:16:18.800
and by splicing in these moments,

00:16:18.800 --> 00:16:21.110
that positively reflect American excess,

00:16:21.110 --> 00:16:23.830
as he learned to do in
advertising right into sequences,

00:16:23.830 --> 00:16:26.810
that favor gut reactions
over heady contemplation,

00:16:26.810 --> 00:16:29.660
we're primed to view both in the same way.

00:16:29.660 --> 00:16:31.660
When our senses are overwhelmed,

00:16:31.660 --> 00:16:33.680
we stop trying to follow the action,

00:16:33.680 --> 00:16:37.260
and instead rely on the content's
impressionistic qualities.

00:16:37.260 --> 00:16:40.240
Using Thompson's thesis, you
could persuasively argue,

00:16:40.240 --> 00:16:41.980
that the chaos of Bayhem,

00:16:41.980 --> 00:16:44.760
primes us to ingest the dominant ideology,

00:16:44.760 --> 00:16:46.410
of American capitalism at hand,

00:16:46.410 --> 00:16:48.750
without the benefit of critical distance.

00:16:48.750 --> 00:16:51.060
So, with everything we've
explored about Bay's work,

00:16:51.060 --> 00:16:54.470
from the pragmatic Box
Office driven ideology,

00:16:54.470 --> 00:16:57.080
to the very American
form of cinematic excess,

00:16:57.080 --> 00:17:00.440
to the purposely
incomprehensible editing style,

00:17:00.440 --> 00:17:01.830
what are we left with?

00:17:01.830 --> 00:17:04.840
Well, we think there's a way
to recognize all of this,

00:17:04.840 --> 00:17:07.120
and still enjoy Bay's films.

00:17:07.120 --> 00:17:10.010
What if we view Bay's work
as a way for us to engage,

00:17:10.010 --> 00:17:12.690
with the realities about
the systems we live within,

00:17:12.690 --> 00:17:15.370
constantly prime must to
stick to the status quo,

00:17:15.370 --> 00:17:17.950
or how ideologies are malleable things,

00:17:17.950 --> 00:17:20.630
that will seep into any gaps they can,

00:17:20.630 --> 00:17:22.670
informing our values and biases.

00:17:22.670 --> 00:17:26.170
In not resembling real life
in any way, shape or form,

00:17:26.170 --> 00:17:28.640
might the films of Michael
Bay be able to teach us,

00:17:28.640 --> 00:17:30.910
how to live a little more thoughtfully,

00:17:30.910 --> 00:17:32.990
in our late capitalist hellscape?

00:17:32.990 --> 00:17:34.530
And in that way, Michael Bay,

00:17:34.530 --> 00:17:37.470
might just qualify as
unintentionally deep,

00:17:37.470 --> 00:17:40.750
whereas it's officially
known "Oopsy Daisy Smart."

00:17:40.750 --> 00:17:42.000
But what do you guys think?

00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:44.460
Is analyzing Bay's films even worth it?

00:17:44.460 --> 00:17:46.470
Or should we listen to the man himself,

00:17:46.470 --> 00:17:49.720
and just accept them as
innocent mindless entertainment.

00:17:49.720 --> 00:17:51.440
Sound off in the comments,

00:17:51.440 --> 00:17:54.710
hit subscribe like it's a
big red demolition button,

00:17:54.710 --> 00:17:56.340
and don't forget to ring that bell.

00:17:56.340 --> 00:17:59.340
It's always a big thanks to our
patrons for all your support

00:17:59.340 --> 00:18:00.530
and be sure to check out,

00:18:00.530 --> 00:18:03.190
the new features available
